{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1PHFC8gFdaEekPAFg9c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. This is a hard formalization, and I don't see a route to a complete proof with the available tools.\n\nThe statement needs two things, and both are substantial:\n\n1. **Computing P(2019).** The proof has to show P(2019) = F₂₀₁₉ − F₁₀₁₀. The standard argument uses an explicit degree-≤1008 interpolant (a Binet- or finite-difference-style identity) and then uniqueness of interpolation through 1009 points. I haven't found a short way to build that interpolant in Lean.\n\n2. **Uniqueness of (j, k).** The iff also requires that F_j − F_k = F₂₀₁₉ − F₁₀₁₀ with j, k ≥ 1 forces (j, k) = (2019, 1010). That needs a growth or Binet-type argument over arbitrary indices, not just the value at one point.\n\nI did not edit `/work/putnam_2019_b5.lean`, so it still contains the original `sorry`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":616,"cache_read_input_tokens":1881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":616,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"589ae9f4-e529-4974-b27c-959ca238778e","uuid":"cfa7c47e-523a-4041-89ac-b67b1706fc0e","timestamp":"2026-10-14T03:02:10.916Z","request_id":"req_011Cg1PHEr3UE51vSKs8LRVD"}
